\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.4}{\ignorespaces Electronic architecture of Abbes: the ESP32 micro-ROS node drives 18 servos through two PCA9685 boards and reads the MPU6050, communicating with the ROS\,2 host over WiFi. Dashed red lines indicate power distribution.}}{15}{figure.caption.23}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.1}{\ignorespaces Firmware data paths: the command path (top) drives the servos at 100~Hz, the sensing path (bottom) publishes the IMU at 50~Hz. Both periodic loops run on the microcontroller's clock.}}{18}{figure.caption.24}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.2}{\ignorespaces micro-ROS bridges the microcontroller to the ROS\,2 graph: a lightweight client on the ESP32 talks to an agent on the host, which connects it to the full DDS network.}}{19}{figure.caption.27}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.3}{\ignorespaces Agent-connection state machine, providing automatic reconnection without rebooting the robot.}}{20}{figure.caption.28}%
